   "line": [
    "Qd2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen swings to d2 to lean on the h6 target — and it rests on a tactical point newer players routinely miss in this system.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 235,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/ppp2p1p/2np1q1p/2b1p3/4P3/2NP1NPP/PPPQ1PB1/R4RK1 w - - 2 11",
   "line": [
    "Qf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "That point is this: the instinctive queen development to f6 walks straight into a knight leap to d5, forking into c7. The natural move is a blunder.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 236,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/ppp2p1p/2np1q1p/2bNp3/4P3/3P1NPP/PPPQ1PB1/R4RK1 b - - 3 11",
   "line": [
    "Nd5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And admire the workhorse on g2 — a single bishop is guarding the knight, defending the h3-pawn, and shielding its own king all at once. That one piece holds the whole position together."
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 250,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/ppp2p1p/2np2qp/2bNp3/4P3/3P1NPP/PPPQ1PB1/R4RK1 w - - 4 12",
   "line": [
    "Qg6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The queen shift to g6 carries a threat against g3, but there's a resource hiding in plain sight. The trigger to spot: when the enemy king and queen both sit on squares a knight could fork, and the forking square is reachable, put the tactical cap on — if the guard on that square can be deflected, the win is already in the pocket.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 304,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/ppp2p1p/2np2qp/2bNp3/1P2P3/3P1NPP/P1PQ1PB1/R4RK1 b - - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "b4"
   ],
   "spoken": "The direct capture fails to a pawn recapture, but the quiet pawn thrust to b4 is the deflector — hitting the bishop that guards the knight far away on the other wing.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
